#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cc92652-6746-583d-bb0e-53991d5f5e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f14cc015-61de-559e-9d6e-4f7e934ed9c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(2x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f14cc015-61de-559e-9d6e-4f7e934ed9c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(2x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494890} :answer-field/id #uuid "a47a029c-812d-5ac8-b753-06da557530dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02602311-35ef-53b7-9d8d-d64db15f3075" :question/math-academy-id "q-341724" :question/problem "Given that $y={(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{(2x)}^{-x}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x}))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x})+x\\cdot ((2x)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{(2x)^{2}})) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x})-1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-{(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1) \\\\  & =-{(2x)}^{-x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
